meta {
  name: Set player inactive
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

put {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/players/:playerId/inactivate
  body: formUrlEncoded
  auth: none
}

params:path {
  playerId: 1
}

body:json {
  sadf
}

body:text {
  This
  
}

body:form-urlencoded {
  inactive: true
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("renderOK: no HX-Reswap header", function() {
    expect(res.getHeader("hx-reswap")).to.be.undefined;
  });
  
  test("renderOK: fragment plus an empty error banner", function() {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.contain('id="app-error"');
    expect(body).to.contain("<tr");
  });
}

docs {
  The only handler currently using the `renderError` / `renderOK` helpers,
  so this request shows what both look like on the wire.
  
  `inactive=true` deactivates and returns the remaining ACTIVE players.
  Anything else (including a missing field) reactivates and returns the
  INACTIVE list — the handler compares against the literal string "true".
  
  The `hx-vals` attribute in player_row.templ looks like JSON, but HTMX
  serialises it as form fields, which is why this body is form-urlencoded.
  
  ## Telling renderOK from renderError
  
  Both return `200 OK`. The status code is not the signal.
  
  | | `renderError` | `renderOK` |
  |---|---|---|
  | `HX-Reswap: none` | present | absent |
  | Body | only `#app-error`, populated | the fragment, then an EMPTY `#app-error` |
  | In the browser | banner appears, page unchanged | page updates, old banner cleared |
  
  The empty banner on success is what clears a stale error. That's why
  `renderOK` renders it at all rather than just the fragment.
  
  ## Edge cases
  
  | Change | Expected |
  |---|---|
  | `inactive: false` | `renderOK`, reactivates, returns the inactive list |
  | id `abc` | `renderError` — see below |
  | id `999999` | `renderOK`, no banner — see below |
  | header `Cookie: user_session_id=nope` | `401` + `HX-Redirect: /login` |
  
  **id `abc`** is the one path in the app that currently produces a
  `renderError`. Assert on it by swapping the tests above for:
  
      expect(res.getHeader("hx-reswap")).to.equal("none");
      expect(res.getBody()).to.contain("Couldn't set player to inactive");
      expect(res.getBody()).to.not.contain("<tr");
  
  Note the message says "set player to inactive" even on the reactivate
  path, because every branch reuses the same string. Send `inactive=false`
  with a bad id to see it.
  
  **id `999999`** takes the SUCCESS path: an UPDATE matching zero rows
  isn't an SQL error, so there's no banner and the table re-renders
  unchanged. `SetPlayerInactive` doesn't return rows affected, so the
  handler has nothing to distinguish it from a real update.
  
  **The unauthenticated case** shows `requireAuth` branching on
  `HX-Request` (set at collection level): with it you get `401` +
  `HX-Redirect`, without it a `303` to `/login`. An explicit `Cookie`
  header overrides Bruno's jar, so it stays logged out even after Login.
}
